<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Debre Markos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Debre Markos is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, in the highlands of Gojjam south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tana">Lake Tana</a>. Founded in the mid-19th century as a royal centre, it was the capital of the kings and governors of Gojjam.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/debre-markos.jpg" alt="Debre Markos" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: O.Mustafin · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DebreMarkos3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Debre Markos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> for Mount of St. Mark, is a city and separate woreda and the administrative seat of the East Gojjam Zone in the Amhara Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. It lies 300 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a>, at an elevation of over 2,400 metres, and covers 73.5 square kilometres. Its climate is subtropical highland, with temperatures averaging between 14 and 20 °C and a rainy season that peaks in July. Mount Chokea, about 60 kilometres to the north at 4,100 metres, is the source of more than 40 rivers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town was originally called Manqwarar, meaning Cold Place, and was founded in 1853 by Dejazmach Tedla Gwalu, then ruler of Gojjam. In 1867 his rival Ras Adal Tessema burned Tedla Gwalu's houses and made the town his capital. In 1869 it became the seat of Ras Adal, who later took the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tekle-haymanot-saint">Tekle Haymanot</a> and the title Negus of Gojjam, and in the 1880s he built the Church of Markos and named the town after it. In 1877 the town suffered further destruction when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>, King of Shewa, captured it. According to Richard Pankhurst, its population then fluctuated greatly with the army: between 20,000 and 40,000 when the negus was in residence, and 5,000 to 6,000 when he was away.</p><p>The explorer Pellegrino Matteucci reached the town on 3 June 1879 and found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tekle-haymanot-of-gojjam">Ras Adal</a>'s restored palace on a hill, with soldiers camped in the valley below. The largest building was 130 metres long, with a thatched roof on 150 columns, and served as a store for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> and weapons and housed ironware workshops. In 1926 the negus's son, Ras Hailu Tekle Haymanot, remodelled the palace in the style of European buildings after a tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and by 1935 the town had postal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> services.</p><p>The Italians reached Debre Markos on 20 May 1936. A revolt in 1938 left it almost besieged until General Ugo Cavallero, with sixty thousand men, crushed it by the end of May. The British Gideon Force and Ethiopian resistance fighters took the Italian fortification on 5 April 1941. In 1968 people of Debre Markos and nearby districts rebelled against a new agricultural income tax. The national army put the rebellion down, but the tax order was scrapped.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2007 national <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 62,497 residents, of whom 97.03 per cent practised Ethiopian Orthodox Christianity. The town's principal church is dedicated to Markos, and the town is known for its traditional church schools nearby, including that of Dima Giorgis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Debre Markos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gojjam was a highland province of farmers and Orthodox monasteries, with its capital at <b>Debre Markos</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gojjam">Kingdom of Gojjam</a></li></ul></section>
